In .NET MAUI, the `Application` class no longer has `OnStart`, `OnSleep`, and `OnResume` virtual methods. Instead, use the **lifecycle events** via `Window`:

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
    {
        var window = new Window(new AppShell());

        window.Created += (s, e) => { /* replaces OnStart */ };
        window.Stopped += (s, e) => { /* replaces OnSleep */ };
        window.Resumed += (s, e) => { /* replaces OnResume */ };

        return window;
    }
}
```

**Key mappings:**
| Xamarin.Forms | .NET MAUI Window Event |
|---------------|------------------------|
| `OnStart()`   | `Window.Created`       |
| `OnSleep()`   | `Window.Stopped`       |
| `OnResume()`  | `Window.Resumed`       |

There are also additional events like `Activated`, `Deactivated`, `Destroying`, and `Backgrounding` for more granular control. See the [MAUI app lifecycle docs](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/maui/fundamentals/app-lifecycle) for platform-specific mapping details.
